The only reason I am playing ASCII right now is for the NAO tournament - 
no other reason. Advantages to playing with the windows version that 
comes with Nethack 3.4.x are:

1. Identification of pets. A white d doesn't tell me much - I have had 
occasions in  the ASCII version where a white d comes bounding towards 
me and I assumed it was my pet when it was not. By using the windows 
gui, you can tell if it is your pet or not because your pets have a 
small heart next to the pet. Also, the size of the pet changes as it 
gains experience.

2. Identification of monsters. I have noted an issue in my last few 
ascension runs that Rodney looks very much like an elvenking - both are 
a magenta @. In fact, on my valk ascension on NAO I blasted the wrong 
person with my wand of death and had to use a second charge to get the 
real wizard. Similar problems exist with ettins and minotaurs (both are 
a light brown H) as well as monsters that are identified with a q.

3. Trap identification. Is that a squeaky board or a pit? They look 
identical in ASCII. I mind moving over a squeaky board much less than a pit.

Those are just things I can think of right off the top of my head.

So the right thing to do isn't to switch to ASCII - the right thing to 
do is to ask tournament sponsors like NAO and devnull to support the 
windows version and dump something that went out of style in 1985.

Lest you believe that people who have adopted the windows gui in nethack 
are soft, I spent several days at the beginning of the month retraining 
myself in ASCII - and then ascended 4 characters (healer, barbarian, 
valkyrie and wizard). So, the prevailing belief that windows players 
can't play nethack on par with ASCII players is ludicrous.

